Git cheat-sheet:
----------------
git log master...playground               - Show only the work your current topic branch has introduced since its common ancestor with master
git log playground --not master           - Excludes commits in the master branch
git log -p                                - Appends the diff introduced to each commit
git log -g                                - Display the reflog designator in the log output as 'ref@{<N>}' or as 'ref@{<timestamp>}'

git branch recover-branch <commit>        - recover the <commit> by creating a new 'recover_branch' at that commit
git fsck --full                           - an utility, which checks Git database for integrity, if you run it with the '--full' option, it shows you all objects that aren’t pointed to by another object
                                            - used to recover the reflog data which are kept in the '.git/logs/' directory
git count-objects -v                      - quickly see how much space you’re using

git diff --cached                         - View the changes you staged for the next commit, --staged is a synonym of --cached
get diff --check                          - Warn if changes introduce conflict markers or whitespace errors

git reflog                                - A log of where your HEAD and branch references have been for the last few months, alias cmd is 'git update-ref'
git reset --hard HEAD@{N}                 - reset HEAD to commit N, effectively discarding all commits and changes made after N, get commit refs with 'git reflog'
git rebase -i HEAD~2^|HEAD~3              - Edit interactively the parent of the last commit with rebase, every commit in the range HEAD~3..HEAD with a changed message and all of its descendants will be rewritten
git show HEAD~|^                          - If you place a ~ (tilde) or a ^ (caret) at the end of a reference, Git will show the parent of that commit (HEAD in this example, HEAD~2 means “the first parent of the first parent” or “the grandparent”)

git stash (push)                          - (Push) a new stash onto Git stack
git stash list                            - List all stacked entries
git stash apply <stash@N>                 - Apply the latest stacked entry (with no given name), or specify the entry from the stack
git stash apply --index                   - Tell the command to try to reapply the staged changes
git stash drop <stash@{N}>                - Run (with the name of the stash) to remove stacked entry
git stash branch <branch> <stash>         - Create and checkout a new <branch> starting from the commit at which the <stash> was originally created, then drop <stash> if <branch> is created successfully

git blame                                 - View what commit was the last to modify each line of any file, annotation tool
git blame -L <line1>,<line2> <file>       - Restrict the output of the annotation to lines <line1> through <line2> of the <file>
git blame -C <file>                       - Analyze the <file> you’re annotating and try to figure out where snippets of code within it originally came from if they were copied from elsewhere

git bisect [options]                      - Binary search through the commit history to identify as quickly as possible which commit introduced an issue
git bisect start                          - Start the binary search
git bisect bad                            - Tell the system that the current commit is broken
git bisect good <commit>                  - Tell bisect when the last known good state was
git bisect bad                            - Tell Git when the re-tested commit is broken and has started the issue
git bisect good                           - Tell Git when the following re-tested commit is working as expected, now Git has all the information it needs to determine where the issue was introduced
git bisect reset                          - Reset your HEAD to where you were before you started the binary search

git submodule add <URL> <name>            - Add an existing Git repository as a submodule of the repository you’re working on with the given <name>, submodules allow you to keep a Git repository as a subdirectory of another Git repository
git submodule init                        - Initialize your local configuration file (.gitmodules - The file that stores the mapping between the project’s URL and the local subdirectory you’ve pulled it into)
git submodule update                      - Fetch all the data from that project and check out the appropriate commit listed in your superproject
                                          - 'git log -1 <module_name>' helps to tshoot uncommited changes in the local module uncommited changes
git push origin :topic                    - use the refspec to delete references from the remote server, because the refspec is <src>:<dst>, by leaving off the <src> part, this basically says to make the 'topic' branch on the remote nothing, which deletes it
git push origin --delete topic            - the newer command syntax

plumbing commands (low-level):
------------------------------
git read-tree                                              - Reads tree information into the index. Optionally, it can merge a tree into the index, perform a fast-forward (i.e. 2-way) merge, or a 3-way merge, with the -m flag
git read-tree --prefix=folder/ -u <remote_branch>          - Pull the 'remote_branch' branch into the 'folder' subdirectory of the current working branch
git diff-tree <tree_branch>                                - See the difference between a specific subdirectory (like a vendor folder) and a separate branch to check for pending merges
git cat-file <type> <object>                               - Output the contents or other properties such as size, type or delta information of one or more objects, use -p for "pretty" output
test/ export-ignore                                        - Test files in a 'test/' subdirectory won’t be included in the archive after running 'git archive' command
git ls-tree                                                - List the contents of a given tree object, like what "/bin/ls -a" does in the current working directory
echo 'a content' | git hash-object -w --stdin              - take the stdin content handed to the 'echo' command and merely return the unique hash key that would be used to store it in the Git database, the -w option then tells the command to not simply return the key, but to write that object to the database
git cat-file -p master^{tree}                              - the 'master^{tree}' syntax specifies the tree object that is pointed to by the last commit on the 'master' branch, in Windows use the 'master^^{tree}' syntax
git update-index --add --cacheinfo <mode> <object> <file>  - artificially add the earlier version of the <file> to a new staging area, the '--add' option must be added because the <file> doesn’t yet exist in the staging area and the '--cacheinfo' option adds the <file> to the working directory from the Git database
                                                             - modes: 100644 = a normal file, 100755 = an executable file, 120000 = a symbolic link
git write-tree                                             - write the staging area out to a tree object, the output is a hash of the tree object
echo 'First commit' | git commit-tree <object>             - create a commit object for the tree object's hash obtained from the previous command
git update-ref refs/heads/<branch> <hash>                  - update the reference 'branch' file with the 'hash' of a selected commit
git symbolic-ref HEAD                                      - read the value of your HEAD
git gc                                                     - the “gc” stands for garbage collect, ask Git to pack up the objects, this process optimizes repository storage by packing objects and cleaning up old, unreachable data
git verify-pack -v .git/objects/pack/pack-<hash>           - look into the packfile and see what Git did to save space, when Git packs objects, it looks for files that are named and sized similarly, and stores just the deltas from one version of the file to the next
git rev-list <commit>                                      - basically the same as the 'git log' command, but by default it prints out only the SHA-1 values and no other information, if you add the '--objects' option, it lists all the commit SHA-1s and also the blob SHA-1s with the file paths associated with them
git rm <pathspec>                                          - remove files matching 'pathspec' from the index, or from the working tree and the index. the command removes only the paths that are known to Git
